{"results":[{"url":"https://www.wral.com/news/ap/4eb6d-fact-focus-false-accusations-and-unverified-claims-quickly-spread-about-election-day-voting/","final_url":"https://www.wral.com/news/ap/4eb6d-fact-focus-false-accusations-and-unverified-claims-quickly-spread-about-election-day-voting/","title":"FACT FOCUS:  False accusations and unverified claims quickly spread about Election Day voting","description":"Election Day was barely underway before misinformation about voting irregularities surfaced online. One actual ballot-counting malfunction Tuesday, in a suburban county north of New York City, gave rise to theories about activities by Democrats or their allies to rig the vote. But there was no in...","language":"en","text":"# FACT FOCUS: False accusations and unverified claims quickly spread about Election Day voting\n\nElection Day was barely underway before misinformation about voting irregularities surfaced online. One actual ballot-counting malfunction Tuesday, in a suburban county north of New York City, gave rise to theories about activities by Democrats or their allies to rig the vote. But there was no in...\n\nPosted\n11/4/2026, 2:18:14 AM\n\nUpdated\n11/4/2026, 6:24:50 AM\n\n* Related\n* Associated Press\n* AP Politics\n* AP Washington news\n* AP Business\n* AP Entertainment\n\nA poll worker closes a secure bag after transferring ballots from their emergency ballot bags in Pelham, N.Y., Tuesday, Nov. 3, 2026. (AP Photo/Seth Wenig)\n\nBy MELISSA GOLDIN and CALVIN WOODWARD — Associated Press\n\nWASHINGTON (AP) — Election Day was barely underway before unverified rumors of voting irregularities surfaced online.\n\nOne actual ballot-scanning malfunction, in Westchester County, New York, in particular gave rise to baseless theories about activities by Democrats or their allies to suppress Republican votes. While there was no indication that episode was engineered to disadvantage either party — “no shenanigans,” said the Republican congressman seeking reelection — the issue delayed the counting of votes until at least Wednesday.\n\nOther WRAL Top Stories\n\nIn Fulton County, Georgia, officials said some voters reported receiving false texts about polling place changes that were not sent by the county. Election watchdogs said it was unclear who sent them and whether the texts were an effort to deceive voters.\n\nA look at some of the claims that made the rounds on social media before the midterm election polls closed Tuesday.\n\n\\_\\_\\_\n\nCLAIM: Issues with voting machines in Westchester County are evidence of Democrats cheating.\n\nTHE FACTS: There is no credible evidence to suggest the ballot-scanning malfunction in the suburban county north of New York City was anything other than a technical problem.\n\nRepublican Rep. Mike Lawler, whose seat was on the line, did not see malfeasance at work. “I want to be clear. There’s no shenanigans going on here,” he said at a news conference.\n\nBut posts were quick to portray the problem as evidence of nefarious activity.\n\n“Nothing to see here? Just front load the early voting & then stop people voting on election day when more Republicans typically vote,” said one post. ”Corrupt (New York Attorney General) Letitia James says she’s ‘monitoring the situation’ which probably means they’re cheating. Flood the zone with our own people,” another post suggested.\n\nBecause of a programming glitch, machines at sites across the county would not accept paper ballots when polls opened. Voters were instructed to put completed ballots in bags that would be safeguarded to be processed later.\n\nNonetheless, Lawler conceded to Democrat Cait Conley late Tuesday. The Associated Press had not called the race as of early Wednesday.\n\n\\_\\_\\_\n\nCLAIM: An FBI raid at a storage locker warehouse in Houston on Monday uncovered more than 20,000 fraudulent ballots for Democratic candidates.\n\nTHE FACTS: This is false. The FBI’s national press office said no raid took place.\n\n“FBI Houston is aware of social media posts claiming the office conducted an election-related ‘raid’ that ‘uncovered fraudulent ballots’ on November 2, 2026,” it wrote in a statement. “That information is false and no election-related operation occurred.”\n\nThe claim was first made by a social media user who describes himself as a “Digital Content Creator for Pro Trump Administration.” His Facebook profile identifies him as a comedian.\n\nPosts by the user included three photos that have nothing to do with the alleged fraud. The first shows an FBI raid in January at the main election facility in Georgia's Fulton County. The second is from a rent listing for Public Storage units in Houston. The third shows ballots in San Francisco collected from an official drop box in October.\n\n\\_\\_\\_\n\nCLAIM: A poll worker in Rogers, Minnesota, who wore a shirt spattered with fake blood that said “Problem Solved,” was mocking the assassination of Charlie Kirk.\n\nTHE FACTS: This is false. The shirt did not refer to the slain conservative activist and the poll worker said she was not making a comment about him.\n\nIn a statement, the city said: “We have spoken with the judge/volunteer in question who has assured us that there was absolutely no political message or intent behind the shirt, that it was worn as Halloween attire, and that they would never be supportive of the apparent message that others have tied to it.”\n\nThe poll worker is a Republican, the city said.\n\nA photo spreading on social media shows the poll worker, whom Rogers officials identified as an election judge, wearing the shirt while she sits at a table in front of a laptop. Election judges are temporary employees “trained to handle all aspects of voting at the polling place.”\n\nThe shirt “did not meet the statutory definition regarding prohibitions against political party or campaign messaging at polling places,” according to the city’s statement. It said the worker would have been asked to remove or cover up the shirt had “the perceived political connection of the shirt been known at the time.” Officials said the poll worker was not sent home to change her shirt, despite claims otherwise on social media.\n\nKirk, a close ally of Republican President Donald Trump, was killed in 2025 during an event at Utah Valley University. Video of Kirk being shot that went viral immediately following the attack showed blood gushing from his neck onto his white T-shirt.\n\nRogers is part of Hennepin County, outside of Minneapolis.\n\n\\_\\_\\_\n\nCLAIM: NBC, even before knowing the outcome of all the congressional races, is going to declare that Democrats won control of the House and Senate.\n\nTHE FACTS: That’s not at all what NBC News said in its guidance on X, which the accuser included in a social media post. NBC News said it was possible, though unlikely, that control of the House and Senate could be known before the end of the night. That guidance correctly summarized the consensus in opinion polling that Democrats went into Election Day with an advantage, without asserting the polling was necessarily right.\n\nThe X post from NBC News said: “Democrats are heavily favored in public polling in the House and in several key Senate races. If those numbers prove accurate, it's possible that NBC News could project control of both chambers on election night. But it’s more likely that determining the winners will take time.”\n\n\\_\\_\\_\n\nAssociated Press writers Tiffany Stanley in Washington and Claire Galofaro in Louisville, Ky., contributed to this report.\n\n\\_\\_\\_\n\nFind AP Fact Checks here: https://apnews.com/APFactCheck.\n\nCopyright 2026 by The Associated Press. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten or redistributed.","author":"MELISSA GOLDIN and CALVIN WOODWARD — Associated Press","published_date":"2026-11-04T02:18:14Z","latency_ms":147.32484700016357,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}